\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Leadership Style:
\f0\b0  You likely lead with a strong desire for fairness and a willingness to admit when you are wrong, making you highly approachable. However, a moderate Machiavellian score (57) suggests you also possess a pragmatic, strategic streak\'97you know how to navigate organizational politics and might occasionally lean on calculated influence to get things done.\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Workplace Biases:
\f0\b0  Because your Humility (82) and Ethical Leadership (79) are high, you may assume your strategic maneuvering is always for the "greater good." This can create a bias where you justify selective information sharing or quiet manipulation because your end goals are well-intentioned.\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Triggers:
\f0\b0  High-stakes projects, rigid hierarchies, or perceived threats to your team's success might activate your moderate risk signals. Under pressure, your empathy (64) might take a backseat, leading to bluntness (Psychopathy 39) or a quiet satisfaction in seeing a difficult rival fail (Sadism 43).\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Context-Sensitive Risks:
\f0\b0  The primary tension in your profile is between transparency and control. In low-trust environments, your protective instincts might cause you to hoard information, sending mixed signals to a team that otherwise sees you as an ethical role model.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\li4620\fi-4620\ri4620\partightenfactor0

